r epsilon to the whole  power of little of one  even for low rate codes and even if we  allow  huge list sizes  one of the consequences of our result is  in an application of the gold rush 11  theorem  for obtaining hardcore predicates  the gold reich 11 theorem can be  restated as follows  in this setting  assume that f is a hard function such  that for circuits of size  s they cannot invert f  with probability more than rho  for the purpose of the talk maybe we can  assume that f is a permutation  now based on f let us define  another one-way function f new that  concatenates the output of f on the  first argument  with that of the second argument so fnu  takes two arguments  we also define a predicate f bread that  also takes two arguments x and r  and outputs the r bit  of the hadamard encoding of x  goldrich levin theorem implies that  given as input the evaluation of  f nu on an input it is impossible  for circuits of size at most roughly  s prime is equal to s over q to compute  the value of f  bread which is really a single bit with  advantage of more than epsilon or random  guessing  for concreteness assume that the  function f  is hard to invert for circuits of size  poly k  and again assume that the probability  that  the circuit c is successful  in inverting with probability at one  over poly k  rho is one over poly k  then gold rank 11 theorem guarantees  that the predicate  f bread cannot be predicted with an  advantage  of more than one over poly k over random  guessing  now suppose we start from a function  f that has a much smaller probability of  successfully  being inverted that is f is a much much  harder function  do we get any improvement on this  epsilon  this is a question that can be asked  no as we  begin with the circuit of size s is  equal to poly k  we really cannot get epsilon less than  one over s  which is equal to one over poly k  and even if you do not follow the  computation  the gist is that lldc's  with query complexity q is equal to  1 over epsilon to the theta of 1  do not help us to obtain a hardcore  predicate  for rho  as even as small as 2 to the minus  square root k  and there is no advantage over a row  with 1 over poly k  one can ask whether it is possible to  obtain a better epsilon  by replacing the hadamard with another  less decodable code  and the answer again is no because by  our theorem for  smaller epsilon this is impossible  even if we use codes with low rate and  large lists  but maybe we could do it via some other  black box technique  instead of this localized decoding  technique  again the answer is no and we show that  this is impossible for a general  definition of black box techniques the  details are in our paper  the proof technique we use here is an  extension  of the technique used for the lower  bound for small epsilon  in the rest of the talk i will focus  on showing our query lower bound on  local list decoding  for the small epsilon case even  for low rate codes and even when  allowing large  list sizes  the main idea in our proof is inspired  from a work of  apple bomitol where they show  limitations on non-deterministic  reductions  that establish hardness amplification  the first step is to reduce to a  slightly different decoding problem  and then show a lower bound on the query  complexity of that problem  just for clarity i have kept a schematic  diagram  of localis decoding on the left pane of  the slide  instead of wanting to decode from  arbitrary words  in this new problem we require  that first a message is sampled  uniformly at random  encoded with the error correcting code  and then xored with a string of length  n string z of length n where each bit in  z  is 1 independently with probability  half minus 2 epsilon we denote this  distribution  by bsc n half minus epsilon or 2 epsilon  now earlier the requirement was to  correctly output each bit of the message  with a certain probability  we change that requirement  to a new requirement of havi